  "summary": "Spatially resolved and single-cell studies now resolve senescence and aging programs within specific human and mouse tissue cell types.",
  "synthesis": "The dominant thread is the resolution of aging and senescence at cell-type and tissue-context scale using single-cell, spatial, and causal-inference approaches. In human ovary, single-cell and spatial multi-omics across menopause identify granulosa-cell remodelling and implicate HEYL in ovarian aging and senescence. In human omental visceral adipose, older adults show more senescent cells than obesity alone explains, with senescent stem/progenitor and mesothelial cells participating in multicellular signaling that impairs organ function and drives inflammation. Spatial causal discovery on Visium transcriptomics of human liver models relationships among senescence markers. In aged mouse liver, p53 loss increases inflammation and metabolic damage, and p53 activation by an MDM2 inhibitor is reported to reverse age-linked changes. A multi-omic spinal cord atlas in mice and humans links age-related changes and senescence markers to specific cell types. What is new is the combination of cell-type resolution with spatial context and perturbation-linked evidence, which frames senescence as tissue- and cell-type-specific rather than uniform.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-haiku-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-11-19"
